WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.590
The district asked me a simple

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.650
question: how many of my pupils walk

3
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.270
more than thirty minutes to school?

4
00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.550
The answer is somewhere.

5
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.500
In my register, two notebooks

6
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.760
and a WhatsApp chat!

7
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.050
Today: the data your school

8
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.390
already keeps, and how to put it

9
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.330
in a table a computer can use.

10
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.060
Data are facts we record.

11
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:26.660
Who came today.

12
00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.230
A pupil's mark.

13
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.100
How much rain fell.

14
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.440
Each one is something we noticed,

15
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.960
written down so we can use it later.

16
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.260
Your school is full of data.

17
00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.010
The register, the mark book, library

18
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.450
cards, the rain gauge in the garden.

19
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.120
Most of it sits on paper, or in a

20
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.080
sheet only one person can open.

21
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.200
A table keeps data in rows and columns.

22
00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.460
Each row is one thing: a

23
00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.980
pupil, a day or a reading.

24
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.220
Each column is one fact about

25
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.600
it, like a name or a mark.

26
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.020
Where they meet is a cell.

27
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.790
Values come in kinds.

28
00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.670
Numbers, like marks or

29
00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.470
minutes, can be added up.

30
00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:19.070
Categories, like a class or how

31
00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:20.790
you travel, can be counted.

32
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.060
Dates and free text need their own care.

33
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.580
You already use a table every morning.

34
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.550
In the register, each row is a

35
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.870
pupil and each column is a day.

36
00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.950
Each cell holds one letter: P

37
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.290
for present, A for absent.

38
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.920
Simple, and easy to count.

39
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:42.800
Quick check!

40
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.690
One cell in my mark sheet says

41
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.450
fourteen slash seventeen: a pupil's

42
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.410
maths and science marks together.

43
00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.140
What's wrong with it?

44
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.750
Two facts in one cell: the computer

45
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.270
sees text, and can't add it up.

46
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.140
Tidy tables follow simple rules.

47
00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.160
One row per thing.

48
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.240
One column per fact.

49
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.180
One value per cell, and no

50
00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.660
total rows among the pupils.

51
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.980
Here's how a sheet goes wrong.

52
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.140
The class is written once,

53
00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.040
then left blank below.

54
00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:21.250
A Total row sits between the classes.

55
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.700
Two marks share one cell.

56
00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.430
People read it easily, but a

57
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.970
computer counts it wrong.

58
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.440
In the app, Mr Bello's mark sheet

59
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.680
looks fine, but the computer gets

60
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.020
three questions wrong.

61
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.930
Tap one fix at a time and

62
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:38.750
watch its answers.

63
00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.210
Which fix turns the most answers right?

64
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:43.980
Your turn.

65
00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.570
I'm making a table of how my pupils come

66
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.930
to school, and how long it takes.

67
00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:51.990
What should one row be?

68
00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:56.220
One pupil per row, with columns for how

69
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.260
they travel and the minutes it takes.

70
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.480
Remember: data are facts we record.

71
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.590
A table keeps them in rows and columns.

72
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.400
And a tidy table is one a

73
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.660
computer can count.

74
00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.290
Try it in class tomorrow: draw

75
00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.390
a table on the board.

76
00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.450
One row for each pupil, by

77
00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:17.850
number, not by name.

78
00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.740
Columns for how they come to

79
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.100
school, and the minutes it takes.

80
00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.450
Then count together: how many walk

81
00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.030
more than thirty minutes?
